## Why This Is Asked

Data governance for multiple data sources with contracts ensures safety, compatibility, and faster onboarding of teams at scale.

## Key Concepts

- Data contracts and schema registry
- Versioned Iceberg schemas and per-source isolation
- Idempotent upserts and SCD2 history
- Contract testing and rollback strategies

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-contract check: validate record against contract version
function validate(record, contract) {
  // check required fields
  for (const f of contract.required) {
    if (!(f in record)) return false;
  }
  // type checks
  for (const [k, t] of Object.entries(contract.types)) {
    if (typeof record[k] !== t) return false;
  }
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you detect contract drift across sources, and what actions would you trigger?
- How would you handle data already ingested when a contract is updated—rollback, versioning, or retroactive validation?

## Why This Question Is Asked
This question evaluates practical design skills for privacy-aware, scalable data ingestion, testing strategies, and architectural decision-making under regional compliance requirements.

## Key Concepts
- Centralized policy registry with dynamic privacy rule enforcement
- Per-record lineage tracking via unique identifiers
- Iceberg upserts with schema evolution support
- Regional isolation and late-data handling (up to 60 minutes)
- Privacy conformance tests and data-quality validation gates

## Code Example
```javascript
// Policy application sketch
function applyPolicy(record, policyRegistry) {
  const policies = policyRegistry.getPolicies(record.region, record.dataType);
  return policies.reduce((r, policy) => policy.transform(r), record);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to design scalable, multi-tenant streaming graph pipelines with strict data contracts, lineage, and compliance controls in modern lakehouse setups.

## Key Concepts

- Real-time streaming patterns: watermarking, lateness windows, fault tolerance
- Idempotent upserts in Iceberg with composite keys (tenant/device/neighbor)
- Schema evolution for nested attributes and backward compatibility
- Per-record lineage and auditability; GDPR delete handling via tombstones
- Multi-tenant isolation, access controls, and governance

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo upsert for edge state
function upsertEdge(existing, incoming) {
  const key = `${incoming.tenant_id}:${incoming.device_id}:${incoming.neighbor_id}`;
  if (!existing || existing.version < incoming.version) return incoming;
  return existing;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test late-arrival handling across tenants?
- What monitoring would you implement to detect cross-tenant data leakage or drift?

## Why This Is Asked
This question assesses the ability to design multi-source ingestion pipelines with basic deduplication and schema handling for an entry-level data engineering position.

## Key Concepts
- Change data capture (CDC) from MongoDB
- Batch ingestion from SFTP
- Canonical schema design vs. source schema
- Idempotent writes and deduplication strategies
- Lightweight testing for data contracts and schema drift

## Code Example
```javascript
function enforceContract(rec) {
  const required = ['customer_id', 'event_ts', 'value'];
  const missing = required.filter(field => rec[field] == null);
  if (missing.length > 0) {
    throw new Error(`Missing required fields: ${missing.join(', ')}`);
  }
  if (typeof rec.value !== 'number') {
    throw new Error('Value must be numeric');
  }
  return rec;
}
```
